 "text": "How does a language model read your words? First, it chops the text into tokens: small pieces of words. Each token becomes a list of numbers, called an embedding, a kind of address for its meaning. Then comes the key idea: attention. Every token looks at every other token, and decides which ones matter most. In the sentence, the cat sat because it was tired, the word it pays the most attention to cat. A transformer stacks this step dozens of times. Each layer refines the meaning, from simple patterns, like grammar, to bigger ideas. Finally, it predicts the next token, as a probability for every possible word. It picks one, adds it to the text, and repeats. That's how it writes, one token at a time.",
